-- ======================================================================
--  Immersive Quest Markers: named NPC spots on the PDA map
-- ======================================================================
--  Puts the NPC's name in the tooltip of a trader / technician / bartender /
--  medic / guide / important-character pin, so hovering one on the PDA map
--  answers "who is that" and not only "what is that". "Trader" becomes
--  "Sidorovich - Trader", and "Important Character" becomes "Wolf - Important
--  Character".
--
--  WHY IT IS THE HINT AND NOT THE ICON. A map spot has exactly two pieces of
--  per-OBJECT state: which location type it wears (iqm_taskspot's business) and
--  its hint string. Everything else -- art, tint, size -- is an attribute of the
--  TYPE, shared by every spot of that type, so the name could never have been
--  drawn on the pin itself. The hint is the only per-NPC text a map spot has.
--
--  HOW IT HOOKS. Vanilla builds these spots in stalker_generic.reset_show_spot,
--  which reads the NPC's `level_spot` out of its logic, picks a location type and
--  a legend string id, and calls level.map_add_object_spot with them
--  (stalker_generic.script:67-125). That function is called through the namespace
--  by modules.reset_generic_schemes (`:219` and `:237`), never captured into a
--  local or a scheme table, so replacing the namespace field is enough for every
--  call site to reach the wrapper -- rung 2 of the hook tree, no unlocalizer and
--  no whole-file override of a script nine other things reset through.
--
--  The wrapper adds nothing to the decision and re-implements none of it: the
--  original runs first, unchanged, and then whatever spot it decided on is looked
--  up on the NPC and its hint is REWRITTEN through level.map_change_spot_hint
--  (level_script.cpp:452-457). So NPCs whose logic says nothing about a spot cost
--  seven registry misses and nothing else, and every rule about WHICH NPCs carry a
--  pin -- goodwill, show_spot, the condlist -- stays vanilla's.
--
--  WHY A COMPOSED STRING IS SAFE TO HAND THE ENGINE. A hint is stored raw and
--  translated at draw time, and CStringTable::translate returns its argument
--  unchanged when the id is not in the table (string_table.cpp:226-234). So a
--  literal "Sidorovich - Trader" renders as itself, and the legend id it replaced
--  ("st_ui_pda_legend_trader") is exactly what to write back to restore vanilla --
--  which is what reverting does below, rather than guessing at English.
--
--  NOTHING HERE OUTLIVES THE MOD. map_add_object_spot leaves the location
--  non-serializable (map_add_object_spot_ser is the one that sets the flag,
--  level_script.cpp:443-450), so these hints are rebuilt from the NPC's logic on
--  every load and no save ever carries one. Uninstalling is enough; there is no
--  revert-before-save promise to keep here, unlike iqm_taskspot's spot TYPES.
--
--  level.map_change_spot_hint IS A SOFT DEPENDENCY, tested at every call rather than
--  assumed. It ships with the modded exes (and so with GAMMA), but this mod runs on bare
--  Anomaly too, and there the wrapper does nothing beyond calling vanilla -- the same
--  shape as the beacon's world2ui_with_depth gate in iqm_core.read_config.
--
--  WHAT IT NAMES. Every pin reset_show_spot puts on a PERSON -- the four trade
--  services, the fast-travel guide, and both important-character pins -- because the
--  question a tooltip on an NPC answers is the same one in every case: who is that.
--  The important-character pin is the one that gains most from it, since "Important
--  Character" is the least informative thing the map says about anybody.
--
--  THE ONE ROW THAT IS NOT HERE is the companion pin (ui_pda2_companion_location).
--  It is not an omission of the same kind: a companion is in the player's own squad,
--  already carded by this mod, and their pin moves with them -- the name is the one
--  thing about that mark nobody is in any doubt about. Adding it is still one row.
--
--  ONE COSMETIC LIMIT, recorded so it is not read as a bug. The tooltip says what
--  the ICON says -- and vanilla gives a technician, bartender or medic whose logic
--  only says `level_spot = trader` the plain trader pin. This mod's nameplates
--  refine that from the NPC's trade catalog (iqm_scan.refine_trader), so a card can
--  read TECHNICIAN over a map pin that says "Trader". Matching the pin is the point:
--  the tooltip explains the mark under the cursor, and a tooltip disagreeing with
--  the glyph it is attached to is worse than one agreeing with a coarse glyph.
-- ======================================================================

-- Location type -> the legend string id vanilla gives it, transcribed from
-- stalker_generic.reset_show_spot. The id is BOTH halves of the job: it is the
-- role word the composed hint is built from, and it is what gets written back
-- when the feature is switched off, so reverting restores the exact string the
-- game would have set and not a translation of it.
--
-- An ARRAY, not a hash, because the order is the answer when an NPC somehow carries
-- two. The rows are in VANILLA'S OWN ORDER -- the order of the if/elseif chain in
-- reset_show_spot, not a grouping of our own -- so "first row wins" reaches the same
-- verdict the game would, for free, rather than being a second opinion about
-- precedence that has to be kept in step with one.
--
-- VIP IS TWO ROWS, not one, and they carry DIFFERENT legend ids: `special` is the
-- faction/story important character and `quest_npc` is the quest one. Both draw an
-- important-character pin and the two strings differ only in capitalisation
-- ("Important Character" / "Important character"), which is exactly why they are
-- transcribed separately -- collapsing them to one id would silently change what one
-- of the two pins says, in a way nobody would spot on screen.
local PINS = {
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_special_location",   legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_special"  },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_trader_location",    legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_trader"   },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_mechanic_location",  legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_mechanic" },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_scout_location",     legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_scout"    },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_barman_location",    legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_barman"   },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_quest_npc_location", legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_vip"      },
	{ spot = "ui_pda2_medic_location",     legend = "st_ui_pda_legend_medic"    },
}

-- The composed hint's shape, as a string id rather than a concatenation here, so a
-- translator can reorder the two halves ("Name - Role" is not a given). %s1 is the
-- NPC's name, %s2 the role word. See st_iqm_spot_named in text/<lang>/st_mcm_iqm.xml.
local FORMAT_ID = "st_iqm_spot_named"

local on = false   -- the feature switch (its own option AND the mod's master one)

-- [npc_id] = the PINS row that NPC's pin matched, recorded on every pass through
-- the wrapper whether or not the hint was stamped. Recording the untouched ones too
-- is what makes the MCM switch work in BOTH directions live: with only the stamped
-- ones known, turning the option on would do nothing until each NPC's logic happened
-- to reset. Bounded by the spot-carrying NPCs seen this session (tens, not
-- thousands), and pruned by the sweep whenever a remembered pin has gone.
local seen = {}

-- Translations are per string id and change only with the language, so they are
-- cached rather than re-fetched per NPC. Cleared by apply_config, which is what
-- runs on an MCM change -- and therefore covers a language change too, the same
-- way iqm_core clears its own header cache in read_config.
local _tr = {}
local function tr(id)
	local s = _tr[id]
	if not s then
		s = game.translate_string(id)
		_tr[id] = s
	end
	return s
end

--- The hint text for one NPC: the composed string, or nil to leave the pin alone.
--
--  Nil rather than a fallback when there is no name: a nameless NPC should
--  keep the plain legend, and returning "" here would blank the tooltip instead.
--  character_name() is the display name (the same one the nameplates use); name()
--  is the engine's section-ish id and is never worth showing, so it is not a fallback.
--
--  The full name, deliberately, and NOT iqm_core's short_ranks abbreviation: that
--  option exists to stop a long rank blowing out a fixed-width CARD, and a tooltip
--  has no width to blow out.
local function composed(obj, legend)
	if not (obj and obj.character_name) then return end
	local name = obj:character_name()
	if not name or name == "" then return end
	return string.format(tr(FORMAT_ID), name, tr(legend))
end

--- Write the right hint onto one remembered NPC's pin. Returns false if the pin has
--  gone, which is the caller's cue to forget it.
--
--  The OFF branch writes the legend ID back, not its translation -- see PINS.
local function stamp(id, row)
	if level.map_has_object_spot(id, row.spot) == 0 then return false end
	if not on then
		level.map_change_spot_hint(id, row.spot, row.legend)
		return true
	end
	local text = composed(level.object_by_id(id), row.legend)
	if text then level.map_change_spot_hint(id, row.spot, text) end
	return true
end

--- The wrapper around stalker_generic.reset_show_spot.
--
--  THE ORIGINAL IS CALLED FIRST AND OUTSIDE THE GUARD. Everything vanilla does here
--  -- removing the old spot, the visible_for_map flag, the goodwill test -- has to
--  happen exactly as it would have, including when it raises: swallowing an error
--  from the game's own code would hide it in a function that eight other resets run
--  through. Our own half IS guarded, because the alternative is that a bad name or a
--  missing binding takes down every NPC's logic reset rather than one tooltip.
local original = nil
local function reset_show_spot(npc, scheme, st, section)
	original(npc, scheme, st, section)

	if not (npc and level and level.map_change_spot_hint) then return end
	pcall(function()
		local id = npc:id()
		for i = 1, #PINS do
			local row = PINS[i]
			if level.map_has_object_spot(id, row.spot) ~= 0 then
				seen[id] = row
				stamp(id, row)
				return
			end
		end
		-- No pin of ours any more (vanilla may have just removed one): forget it, so
		-- the sweep does not keep asking after an NPC that no longer carries one.
		seen[id] = nil
	end)
end

--- Pulled by iqm_core at the end of read_config, like every other module's.
--
--  Re-stamps or reverts every remembered pin, so the option takes effect on the pins
--  already on the map rather than at each NPC's next logic reset -- which for a hub
--  the player is standing in is "not this visit".
function apply_config()
	local C = iqm_core and iqm_core.config and iqm_core.config()
	local want = (C and C.enabled and C.map_spot_names) and true or false
	local was = on
	on = want
	_tr = {}   -- covers a language change as well as a switch

	if was == on then return end
	if not (level and level.map_change_spot_hint) then return end
	for id, row in pairs(seen) do
		if not stamp(id, row) then seen[id] = nil end
	end
end

--- Install the wrapper.
--
--  THE SAVED ORIGINAL LIVES ON stalker_generic, NOT IN A LOCAL HERE, and that is what
--  makes installing idempotent. Wrapping a wrapper would compose the hint out of a
--  composed hint -- "Sidorovich - Sidorovich - Trader", growing by one name every time
--  -- so the second install has to find the first. A local flag cannot see one left by
--  an earlier READ of this file, and loading a save re-reads .script files; a field on
--  the module being patched survives exactly as long as the patch does. So the vanilla
--  function is taken from there when it is already recorded, and this call REPLACES the
--  live wrapper rather than stacking on it.
function on_game_start()
	if not (stalker_generic and stalker_generic.reset_show_spot) then return end
	original = stalker_generic.iqm_spothint_original or stalker_generic.reset_show_spot
	stalker_generic.iqm_spothint_original = original
	stalker_generic.reset_show_spot = reset_show_spot
end
